nt)\n        \n        if current == end:\n            break\n        \n        # If we found a shorter path to this node already, skip\n        if d > dist.get(current, float('inf')):\n            continue\n        \n        # Explore neighbors\n        if current in graph:\n            for neighbor, weight in graph[current].items():\n                new_dist = d + weight\n                if neighbor not in dist or new_dist < dist[neighbor]:\n                    dist[neighbor] = new_dist\n                    prev[neighbor] = current\n                    heapq.heappush(pq, (new_dist, neighbor))\n    \n    # If end is not reachable\n    if end not in dist:\n        return (None, None)\n    \n    # Reconstruct path\n    path = []\n    current = end\n    while current != start:\n        path.append(current)\n        current = prev.get(current)\n        if current is None:\n            break  # Shouldn't happen if reachable\n    path.append(start)\n    path.reverse()\n    \n    return (dist[end], path)\n```\n\nI'm carefully examining the edge case where start equals end. The current implementation needs to handle this scenario precisely. When the start and end nodes are identical, we should return a distance of 0 and a path containing just that single node. This requires special handling in the algorithm's logic.\n\nThe key challenge is ensuring the algorithm correctly manages the case where the starting point is also the destination. I'll need to modify the function's initial checks to explicitly handle this unique scenario before running the main Dijkstra's algorithm.",
